WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:10.920
My name is Jim Cloos. One of my primary tasks
is drafting the European Council conclusions,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:15.699
so at the level of the heads of state and government,
and that is why I am also sitting in

00:00:15.699 --> 00:00:21.970
the room when the meetings go on so that I
can quickly react when leaders want changes

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:27.480
in the text.
It happens quite frequently that they change

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:33.300
or they add things and we have to be very
quick in sort of capturing what they want

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.690
to express in those conclusions which is then
translated into all the official languages

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:39.690
of the European Union.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:43.090
'BRUSSELS, DECEMBER 2013 - EUROPEAN COUNCIL'

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:45.339
It's a very complex business of course because

00:00:45.639 --> 00:00:51.479
we are talking about 28 heads of state and
government. So it's not an anodyne meeting,

00:00:51.479 --> 00:00:55.859
it's an important meeting, it's a meeting
where decisions are taken. So there is a very

00:00:55.859 --> 00:01:01.190
elaborate way of preparing this, under the
authority of Mr Van Rompuy of course. The

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:07.280
European Council is a club, you feel it in
the room. There is a sense of responsibility.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.210
The leaders - the heads of state and government
- want agreements, they want things to move

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:15.970
forward. They don't want to come out and say
we disagreed on everything. So it is difficult

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:19.850
work, there is a lot of tension, there is
a lot of fighting, that is democracy after

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:25.430
all. I mean democracy is about ideas, very
often controversial ideas, then you sit around

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:30.030
the table and you try to find a solution.
And that's what it's all about.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:37.030
FROM CRISIS, OPPORTUNITY
Europe's Story

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:48.460
It has been a massive crisis, and probably
the biggest one, but not the first one and

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:53.520
I would even say it's not the last one, because
that's the way things go. Very often the Union

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.390
actually emerges stronger from crises. This
has been a pattern in history. Now this has

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:03.479
been a very serious crisis, and it has been
extremely painful, but if you step back and

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:07.729
look at what has been done, I think it's a
major step forward. And I'm utterly convinced

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:10.000
that the Union will emerge stronger.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.800
Today we are still in crisis,

00:02:19.049 --> 00:02:24.849
an economic one, a social one, etc. 
But we're no longer in a situation

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:29.420
where it's like we were standing before the abyss,
as if Europe could explode from one day to the next.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:36.060
And I think that these political motives, this profound
determination to continue this adventure together,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:41.700
has its roots in the long history
of our continent

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:50.300
with its wars and attempts at peace.
So, it's part of history that starts, perhaps,

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:56.300
with Napoleon or Bismarck, but also,
of course, with 1914 and 1939-45.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.400
'YPRES MENIN GATE MEMORIAL'
To soldiers killed in World War I

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:16.709
In the battlefields around Ypres, 600,000
young men were killed. 600,000. So this was

00:03:16.709 --> 00:03:24.700
100 years ago, and we thought after the First
World War that we could organise a lasting

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:31.680
peace. Unfortunately, we only started the
process of reconciliation in Europe after

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:39.600
a Second World War with even more victims.
So that's the very beginning of the European

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:46.800
Union, that is really the European construction:
built on the graves of millions of people.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.900
And it was right there, on the ruins
and the debris of that Europe,

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:14.200
that a certain number of countries got together
to say: 'Right, never again.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:20.000
We no longer want to kill ourselves, between 
the Germans and French, and all the others,

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:24.670
and we want to start something new'. So,
the moment of European foundation

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:31.700
is really based on that idea of peace,
which was very strong at the time

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.030
for statesmen, the elites, but also
for the people. It's still felt today,

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:42.360
at least by the leaders, that the Europe
we have now and that we want to build,

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:49.460
that its reason for being, at its
deepest level, is still peace;

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.400
good relations between countries.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.880
'BERLIN, 1989 - FALL OF THE WALL'

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.080
The Fall of the Berlin Wall was one of

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:18.680
the most beautiful moments of European history
in the 20th Century. It was a time when

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:24.620
Europe became conscious once again,
in a way, of its unity.

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:33.860
The 10 new member states came to us, to the
European Union, as a result of the end of

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:40.720
the Cold War. So they lived during decades
still in the atmosphere, in the climate of

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:49.870
the post-war period.
We are now negotiating also with Serbia, with

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:56.430
Montenegro, most likely with other countries.
So the European Union is still a dynamic process,

00:05:57.030 --> 00:06:03.900
a process that is going step by step covering
the whole territory of the European continent.

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:08.200
It is still an attractive project, and people
tend to forget this.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:19.780
Enlargement has been, I think, a massive achievement.
Let me say this personally, when I arrived

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:27.470
in Brussels in 1985 we were 10 member states;
we are now 28. That's a revolution. And actually

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:32.580
if you ask the countries outside of the European
Union, a lot of them want to get in.

00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:39.380
Look at the debate in Ukraine for instance.
It allows you to develop law, a legal system,

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:44.440
a system of an internal market which functions,
and all of that. If you think that now we

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:51.120
have countries which 20 years ago belonged
to the Soviet Union, and they are now members.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.060
One of them, Lithuania, has just had the Presidency
for 6 months. I think it's a miracle.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.500
This revolution which we saw, which turned
even bloody, made very clear that Ukraine

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:12.210
is different than Belorussia or Russia, that
Ukrainian people are more pro-European. But

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:19.210
still the country is not unanimous on this.
I think that we cannot exclude Russia from

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:25.570
any developments in Europe, and that Russia
is a neighbour, and anyway Russia is an economic

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:32.570
partner, anyway Russia is a main energy supplier,
and Russia is a huge country, partly in Europe.

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:40.490
Still the time is very turbulent.
But the people understood that they will not

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:47.170
allow politicians to mismanage the country,
they want to be directly involved in the decisions

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:54.030
of their country's future. And this is also
an example for us Europeans; if governments

00:07:54.030 --> 00:08:01.030
with corruption, with mismanagements are overshooting
their legal responsibilities, people in such

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:07.760
countries can take their own decisions, and
try to influence the future themselves.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:12.530
We need to give no illusions, neither for the
new government, neither for Ukrainian people

00:08:12.530 --> 00:08:18.340
that the road will be difficult. We can only
help, but everything that they started to

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:21.510
do, they need to finish themselves.

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:24.310
When we saw the waving of the European flag,

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:31.710
it was a very strong signal that European
values are still attractive, because what

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:40.030
were they defending? The values of democracy,
the rule of law and human rights

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:52.420
against a regime that uses manipulation, lies, blackmail
and at the end even violence.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:05.990
Real change cannot be imposed quickly. A dictator
can impose something, but it is not going to last.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:13.700
This is the beauty of the European
Union. Every member state, whatever the present

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:18.610
circumstances - there might be some economic
or political or other difficulties - takes

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:25.610
over the Presidency for six months. Of course
it doesn't rule the Union for six months;

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:31.040
it chairs one of the institutions of the European
Union, a very important institution: which

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:37.040
is the Council. It renders a service for six
months. So now for 6 months I am almost a

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:43.570
Greek civil servant in a way because I work
directly for and with the Greek ministers

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:50.150
and the Greek senior civil servants. So a
presidency is not somewhere in the void, and

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:53.810
has to get up in the morning and say: 'What
I am going to do with Europe tomorrow?' It

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:56.010
is part of a system.

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:03.040
I think we should not be stupidly, naively

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:07.440
optimistic, but I still believe that Europe
is a continent which has a lot to offer. The

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:13.040
Union does a lot of things. Free movement,
circulation. You don't have to stop at the

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:18.300
borders. Our students can travel, can live
in other countries. All of this is now taken

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:23.940
for granted. And I think we should encourage
young people to grab their chances and to do it.

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:41.550
The Union will be what the people of
Europe will make out of it. And there are

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:47.190
ways of influencing, there is a way of making
your views known nowadays. So you should do

00:10:47.190 --> 00:10:51.890
that rather than constantly lamenting and
saying that things are going badly. I think

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:53.300
Europe has a huge potential.

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:03.500
- Produced by the Council of the European Union -

